// (1) DEBUGGING CYCLE on a failing method:
//   Reproduce: total() returns 0 for a non-empty cart.
//   Hypothesis: the stream filter is dropping all items.
//   Inspect: set a breakpoint on the filter, see `price > 0` rejects 0-priced
//     items (a free item). Confirm: the hypothesis is correct.
//   Fix: change the predicate to `price >= 0` (free items count).
//   Verify: add a regression test with a free item.
public Money total(List<OrderLine> lines) {
    return lines.stream()
        .filter(l -> l.price().signum() > 0)   // BUG: drops free items
        .map(OrderLine::subtotal)
        .reduce(Money.ZERO, Money::plus);
}

// (2) LEVELED, STRUCTURED LOGGING with SLF4J.
private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(Cart.class);

public void checkout(Cart c) {
    log.info("checkout started");                          // always on
    log.debug("cart contents={}", c.items());              // on-demand, prod-safe
    try {
        pay(c);
    } catch (PaymentException e) {
        // ERROR with CONTEXT (not just the message) -> searchable later.
        log.error("checkout failed userId={} amount={}", c.userId(), c.total(), e);
        throw e;
    }
}
// Structured fields (userId, amount) make logs queryable; the exception `e`
//   as the last arg prints the full stack trace — the key clue.

```java
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

public class OrderService {
    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(OrderService.class);

    public void placeOrder(Order order) {
        log.info("Order placed: orderId={}, customerId={}, total={}",
                 order.id(), order.customerId(), order.total());
        try {
            payments.charge(order);
        } catch (PaymentException e) {
            // Stack trace + business context = a line you can debug from months later
            log.error("Payment failed: orderId={}, customerId={}",
                      order.id(), order.customerId(), e);
            throw e;
        }
    }
}
```

Months later, when a customer disputes a charge, this single line tells you the order, the customer, and exactly what failed.

